cartridge "STARFOLK/AHOY! DEC 1985/1985"

' STARFOLK
' A ColecoVision game based on "Gypsy" (title screen: GYPSY PILOT), Ahoy!
' December 1985 (Commodore 64 BASIC, disk files GYPSY, GYPSY VIDEO (its
' machine-code sprite mover) and GYPSY PLANETS (the texts); the listing
' names no author). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by
' Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game: you pilot a travelling family's starship. Fly alongside one of
' four drifting planets and match its speed to enter a stationary orbit:
' gas giants refuel the ship, rocky planets top up the supplies (to half).
' Thrust burns fuel, supplies run down with time; running out of either
' ends the voyage. Press the button in orbit to land (rocky planets, and
' the moons and space stations some gas giants have). On the ground pick
' what the family does from a menu: look around, ask questions, put on a
' show, offer to tinker, dicker, ask for help, take what you need, go
' back, or resign. Every choice risks a mishap that costs a crew member;
' finds must be taken or bought to add to the cargo. Lose too many (fewer
' than 5 left) and the head of the family removes you as pilot. Lead the
' family to 25 worlds to win.
'
' Kept from the original (orig/gypsy.bas): the three voyage levels and
' their numbers (DL = 3 + rnd 6 + level - HB, EF = 6 + rnd 6 - level - HB,
' 1-3 finds a world, three mishaps a world in order, the third sends you
' back); every menu action's rule (lines 432-587), find values 1 + rnd 10
' x level - rnd(12 - DL); supplies refilled to full on landing, to half
' in orbit of a rocky planet, fuel to full at a gas giant; gas giants have
' a 12% chance of a moon (3% a space station of humans or giant bees);
' every world type is used once per voyage per kind (10 uninhabited, 10
' human, 10 alien); the 33-name family with a random head; the world
' texts (edited, see texts.py).
'
' Liberties: the original's text called the family "gypsies" and leaned on
' stereotypes (theft, begging); here they are a neutral starfaring family
' and the texts are rewritten where needed (texts.py lists what changed);
' invented world names. The orbit is rebuilt: the ship has momentum and
' drag, an orbit locks after 20 frames close to a planet at nearly its
' speed (the C64's machine code for this was not read). Gas giants show
' their moon or station as a small sprite (the C64 said "no surface for
' landing" only after you pressed). A gas giant with nothing to land on
' is no longer replaced when you press (pressing again and again counted
' as worlds visited and could win the game without landing); after a
' landing the world drifts away and a new one comes in from the edge of
' the screen, so the ship must fly to every world. The goal is 25 worlds
' landed on (the C64 ended after 29 worlds generated, the first four
' included). Find values are at least 1 (the C64's formula could go
' negative); DL and EF stay at least 1. No PLAY AGAIN/QUIT page.
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2). Stick: thrust in eight directions (in
' orbit, a push held for 1/6 second leaves the orbit). Button: land, while in orbit. On the
' ground and in menus: up/down choose, a button chooses.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.

